## ***The Senate passed a 494-page energy bill by stuffing it inside an unrelated House bill. Now the House plans to pass it in 40 minutes, with no amendments, before the new Congress arrives.***

> **Corporatocracy** *(noun)* — a system of government in which corporations, financial institutions, or other business entities hold effective political power, either by directly controlling the state or by exerting decisive influence over the officials and institutions that do.

> **Uniparty** *(noun, informal)* — a term for the Republican and Democratic parties acting as one governing class: fighting loudly over culture-war issues in public, while cooperating quietly to protect donors, corporate interests, and their own power.

On Friday, I showed you how the Senate passed a 494-page energy bill by stuffing it inside a tax bill for sexual-abuse survivors. If you missed it, start here: [Wait Until After the Election: How the Uniparty Slipped a 494-Page Energy Bill Past You](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/uniparty-energy-bill-lame-duck-bait-and-switch/).

That was step one. This is step two.

The bill, the Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act (BAAJA), now goes back to the House. And the plan for getting it through the House is just as slick as the plan for getting it through the Senate.

### Where things stand

The Senate passed the bill 62-33 late Thursday, November 19. Because the Senate replaced the House’s original bill with its own text, the House has to vote again before it can go to the President.

House leaders plan to take it up when the House returns the week after Thanksgiving, and according to the Washington Times, they want to pass it **without changes**. Any change at all would send it back to the Senate with only weeks left in the year.

Speaker Mike Johnson added the permitting bill to his list of year-end priorities in a letter urging Republicans to return after Thanksgiving. House Majority Leader Steve Scalise says it will “cut red tape, create jobs, and lower costs for families.”

### The shortcut: 40 minutes, no amendments

Speaker Johnson has said he’ll try to pass the bill under a procedure called **suspension of the rules**.

Here’s what that means, according to the Congressional Research Service:

- **Debate is capped at 40 minutes.** Forty minutes for a 494-page bill that rewrites how America approves pipelines, power lines, drilling, mines, and data centers.
- **No amendments are allowed from the floor.** Nobody gets to offer a fix.
- **It takes a two-thirds vote** of the members present to pass.

Suspension is usually used for less controversial bills, like naming post offices. So why use it here? The Washington Times reports that Republicans are having attendance problems that make it hard to pass the “rule” they would need for a normal, simple-majority vote. Johnson has already told Republicans to show up and vote. Heatmap reports that since the midterms, many Republicans who lost their seats have “checked out,” and House leadership canceled several votes last week after too few members showed up to hold the party line. In plain English: the majority can’t count on its own members to be there, so it’s reaching for a shortcut that needs Democratic votes.

That’s the Uniparty at work. The fastest way through the House is a deal that needs both parties.

### The fixes that never got a vote

Remember the “manager’s amendment”? That was a package of changes the bill’s own authors negotiated to answer complaints, mostly from Native American tribes, about weakened consultation on projects that could affect their lands and sacred sites. One change would have required tribal consultation on all federally funded projects that might affect tribal resources, not just projects that need a federal permit.

It never got a vote.

Here’s how it died, according to Heatmap News. Republicans blocked an amendment from Oregon Democrats Jeff Merkley and Ron Wyden to ban data centers on federal lands. Merkley and Wyden then blocked the vote on the manager’s package. So the Senate passed the bill with **no amendments at all**.

Sen.&nbsp;Lisa Murkowski (R-Alaska), who negotiated tribal protections in the package, said it was “blocked for reasons unrelated to the Tribal protections at the eleventh hour.”

Now the bill’s lead negotiator, Sen.&nbsp;Mike Lee (R-Utah), says those fixes could be added to another bill or passed on their own. Sen.&nbsp;Shelley Moore Capito (R-W.Va.) warned that adding them in the House could “create more problems.”

So the promise is: pass it now, fix it later.

### Both parties are fighting. Both parties are dealing.

This isn’t a one-party story. Look at who’s doing what:

- **Senate Democrats split.** Only 15 of the 47 senators who caucus with Democrats (14 Democrats and one independent) voted for final passage. Senate Democratic Leader Chuck Schumer voted yes when the Senate swapped in the energy bill’s text on November 18.
- **Democrats attack Democrats.** Sen.&nbsp;Ruben Gallego (D-Ariz.) accused Merkley of blocking the improvements to make the bill “so unpopular that it will die in the House.”
- **Merkley fires back** at the bill itself: “There is enormous concern that this bill puts the pedal to the floor on data centers across the country.”
- **House Democrats split too.** The centrist New Democrat Coalition has endorsed the bill. Rep.&nbsp;Scott Peters (D-Calif.): “We should make sure this bill gets across the finish line to lower costs and emissions.” Rep.&nbsp;Melanie Stansbury (D-N.M.): “this is not the bill, this is not the time and this [is] not what the American people asked for.” The Congressional Progressive Caucus has formally opposed it, and Rep.&nbsp;Jared Huffman (D-Calif.) is urging colleagues to vote no.
- **House Democratic Leader Hakeem Jeffries** has spoken only vaguely about issues to address. His office did not respond to the Washington Times.
- **Republicans aren’t united either.** Sens. Mike Rounds (R-S.D.) and Josh Hawley (R-Mo.) voted no.

### The clock they’re racing

Here’s the real deadline. This Congress’s term ends January 3. That’s 41 days from today. After that, Democrats take control of the House and Senate.

President Trump urged senators to pass the bill before that happens. Some Democrats want to wait for their new majority to negotiate a better deal. Others argue Trump may not renegotiate, so they should take the deal now and push for improvements later.

And there’s another clock. Government funding runs out on December 11. Permitting has to share a short December with a shutdown fight.

### Trump praised it, then undercut part of it

President Trump praised the bill’s Senate passage. “It’s one of the biggest things that you will see. It’s a big deal,” he said, adding that it’s “projected to put at least $1,300 a year back in the pockets of hardworking Americans.” He didn’t say whose projection that is.

Earlier this month, he also said he still wouldn’t green-light wind energy projects, calling wind turbines “a major loser.”

That matters because of how Democrats got to yes. Last December, the Interior Department halted the five Atlantic coast offshore wind projects that were already under construction. Two top Senate Democrats called the permitting talks dead unless the administration reversed course, and Democrats pushed for “ironclad” language to keep any administration from killing a project’s permits after they’re issued.

They got some of that. Section 1401, the so-called “forever permits” section, says an agency “shall not take any new action that would revoke, rescind, withdraw, terminate, suspend, vacate, remand, reopen, materially amend, or materially alter” a project’s federal permits, with some exceptions, including a court order or fraud by the permit holder.

Here’s the catch. That protection only kicks in once a permit exists. Nothing in that section makes any administration issue a new one. So the President can sign a bill that protects permits and still refuse to approve the wind projects that need them. He’s already told you he plans to.

### Who pays for the data centers?

Supporters say the bill makes data centers pay their share of grid costs. A Grid Strategies analysis cited by Sen.&nbsp;Martin Heinrich’s (D-N.M.) office estimates data centers would contribute about $55 billion toward transmission costs over the next decade, roughly $35 billion more than under current policy.

Critics like Merkley and Wyden say the bill speeds up data center construction, including on public lands. “I don’t want to fill up our treasured public lands with all these data centers,” Wyden said.

Both can be true. The bill can charge data centers more **and** clear the way for more of them to be built. Either way, the people who build data centers are the ones with a seat at this table.

**What about Texas?** If you live in Texas, most of that $55 billion debate isn’t about your grid. About 90% of the state’s electricity flows through ERCOT, which largely operates outside federal transmission oversight, and the bill keeps ERCOT exempt from the new federal power-line siting authority. Whether data centers pay their way on the Texas grid is mostly up to Austin.

Texans are already paying for the buildout. Data centers can be charged the direct cost of connecting to the grid, but ERCOT spreads the cost of the broader transmission buildout across all customers. Since 2022, transmission and distribution fees have climbed to 39% of the average residential bill, up 11 points. Earlier this year, Texas Public Utility Commission Chairman Thomas Gleeson said “the majority” of the new grid build “is for these large industrial data center customers.”

Data centers also get a tax break the rest of us don’t. Under a 2013 state law, qualifying data centers are exempt from the 6.25% state sales tax on their equipment and the electricity they use. The Texas Comptroller’s office estimates that will cost the state about $3.2 billion in sales tax revenue over two years. At a July hearing, state senators questioned whether the Comptroller is checking that companies meet the requirements. Nobody has published a full tally of what data centers pay toward the Texas grid compared with what they receive in breaks.

Austin has already started. In 2025, the Legislature passed, and Gov.&nbsp;Greg Abbott signed, Senate Bill 6, which requires new power users of 75 megawatts or more to pay part of the cost of connecting to the grid and allows ERCOT to order them to cut back during grid emergencies. In August, Abbott ordered an audit of every data center in ERCOT’s connection line before any more could be approved.

The next round falls to a new team. When the Legislature convenes January 12, Democrats will control the Texas House for the first time in more than two decades, Republicans will still hold the Senate, and Gov.-elect Gina Hinojosa will be in office. BAAJA’s changes to federal environmental reviews would still apply to Texas projects that require federal permits, such as interstate pipelines.

### My take

*This section is my opinion.*

I’m not telling you this bill is all bad. Parts of it may help. But how it’s being passed tells you who it’s for.

Remember March 2010? Then-Speaker Nancy Pelosi, pushing the Affordable Care Act, told a room of county officials: “We have to pass the bill so that you can find out what is in it, away from the fog of the controversy.” Her defenders say she meant the good parts would show once the shouting stopped. Either way, she was talking about one bill being passed by a Democratic majority. But she said the quiet part out loud, and it describes the usual order of business in Congress, no matter who’s in charge.

Republicans hammered Pelosi for it for years. Then in December 2017, Senate Republicans passed a 479-page tax bill just before 2 a.m., with last-minute changes handwritten in the margins. Different party, same playbook.

This time, it isn’t even one party. If BAAJA passes the House, it will pass as a coalition: a borrowed bill in the Senate, a blocked amendment process, and a 40-minute, no-amendments vote in the House, timed for the last weeks before a new Congress takes over. Party-line or bipartisan, the saying holds. Pass it now, find out what’s in it later, fix it later.

Texas can’t wait on Washington for this one. Whatever BAAJA does about data center costs on the federal grid, it mostly stops at the ERCOT line. When the new Legislature meets January 12, it should move fast on three things: make data centers pay for the grid buildout they trigger, not just their own hookups; put the more-than-$1-billion-a-year sales tax break on the table and audit who’s getting it; and finish the Senate Bill 6 rules before the next wave of data centers gets in line. Texans are already paying more for their power. They shouldn’t be paying for someone else’s server farm too.

That’s the cycle. Ram it through, skip the debate, and when the problems show up, run the next campaign on promising to fix them. I’m tired of it, and I suspect you are too.

When both parties agree to skip the debate, the public is the only one left out of the room.

## Call to Action

This fight is in two places now. The U.S. House vote could come as soon as the week after Thanksgiving. The Texas Legislature meets January 12. You have time to be heard in both, but not much.

1. **Call your U.S. Representative** at the Capitol switchboard, (202) 224-3121. Ask one question: *“Will you vote to pass a 494-page bill with 40 minutes of debate and no amendments?”*
1. **Ask for the fixes first.** If the tribal-consultation fixes and other changes are worth passing “later,” ask why they can’t be in the bill now.
1. **Texans: contact your state representative and state senator.** Find both by entering your address in “Who Represents Me?” on Texas Legislature Online, [capitol.texas.gov](https://capitol.texas.gov/). Ask them to make data centers pay for the grid buildout they trigger, not just their own hookups, and to put the data center sales tax break up for review. Ask one question: *“Will you file or support a bill that moves grid costs off residents and onto the data centers driving them?”*
1. **Watch the vote.** I’ll publish how every Texas member of the U.S. House votes when it happens.
1. **Share this.** Most people still don’t know this bill exists, or that they’re already helping pay for the data center boom.

